<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A thirteen-generation story, from a Yorkshire tannery yard in the storms of the seventeenth century &mdash; through the woollen villages of the Aire valley, across the Atlantic to the Saskatchewan dust, and west again into the Alberta oilfields and the Peace Country. Where the records and the inherited genealogy disagree, the records win; see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a> for what is confirmed, what was overturned, and what is still open.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-word-they-never-named"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>The word they never named</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Yorkshire → Saskatchewan → Alberta · c. 1610 to today</p><p>Every long family has a moment when the line nearly ends. For the Haistes, that moment came in the middle of the seventeenth century, when armies marched across Yorkshire during the English Civil War, plague swept the country in 1665, and the worst winters of the Little Ice Age killed two harvests running. In the middle of all of it, a young Yorkshire tanner named Richard kept his household alive long enough to baptize a son in the parish church of Barwick-in-Elmet in the spring of 1641. The son was named George. Three and a half centuries later, that single thread of survival had become an Alberta family of dozens &mdash; a thread that runs from Yorkshire fields through the long transformation of Yorkshire textiles, across the Atlantic in a steamship, into Saskatchewan dust, and west again into the Alberta oilfields and the towns of the Peace Country.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>They came through.</p></aside><p>The Haistes have no single word for what carried them through. You see it in Richard, who survived a civil war. In a Yorkshire clothier named Joseph who married Hannah Padget at Calverley Church in 1802 and raised seven children outside Idle. In Ernest, who left England as a young man for a quarter-section of Saskatchewan grass he had never seen. In Sydney, who pulled his family out of a Depression dust storm and pointed them toward Alberta. In Jim, who lived through nine decades of change and buried his own son.</p><p>This is their story, told one generation at a time, across the world they had to come through.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-three-centuries-in-the-yorkshire-parishes-c-1610"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>Three centuries in the Yorkshire parishes (c. 1610 &ndash; c. 1780) &mdash; tanners, butchers, and the pull of the city</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1610 &ndash; c. 1780 · the Yorkshire parishes around Leeds</p><p>Richard Haiste was born around 1610, almost certainly in one of the small Yorkshire parishes clustered around Leeds &mdash; Barwick-in-Elmet, Hunslet, Tadcaster &mdash; where his surname appears in the earliest surviving church records. By trade he was a yeoman and a tanner &mdash; hides turned, through weeks of soaking in oak-bark pits and patient scraping, into the leather everyone depended on: filthy, skilled, respected work. And he lived through the most violent stretch of English history since the Wars of the Roses: Yorkshire was a battlefield through the worst of the Civil War, and on top of war came the Great Plague of 1665 and the Little Ice Age, which made his winters colder and his harvests thinner than any in living memory.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch01-tanner-and-son.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Yorkshire tanner and his young son in the fields" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1536" /><figcaption>A tanner and his boy in the Yorkshire fields &mdash; the trade, and the world, Richard was born into. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch04-yorkshire-village-lane.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A lane through a Yorkshire village of stone and thatched cottages" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1536" /><figcaption>A village lane in the Aire valley &mdash; the rural Yorkshire the family was beginning to leave. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He handed off a living family to the next generation.</p></aside><p>Of his home life the registers hold almost nothing &mdash; his wife was probably Elizabeth Haigh, by family reconstruction rather than register entry, and many of the pre-1653 parish books were lost when armies ransacked the churches. One son is documented for certain: George, baptized in the parish church of Barwick-in-Elmet in the spring of 1641. When Richard died, around 1670, he had done the most important thing a seventeenth-century man could do: handed off a living family to the next generation. His name will never appear in any history book.</p><p>That son grew up in the middle of the war and lived to see England stabilize. George Haiste Sr. stayed in the parishes around Barwick-in-Elmet as a tenant farmer and a butcher, married a woman named Mary, her surname lost, and raised what parish reconstructions put at ten or more children. He died in 1709, at sixty-eight, having steadied the family Richard had handed him &mdash; the Haiste name would never again be at risk of dying out in Yorkshire.</p><p>His son George (c. 1667&ndash;1743) carried the same trades through the same Hunslet&ndash;Barwick parishes, in the last quiet decades before the mills and railways that would, a century on, pull his great-grandsons out of the countryside for good. He lived in the breath right before it. And around 1715 another George was born into the line &mdash; <em>Georg Haist</em> in some registers, <em>George Haiste</em> in others, the name itself still in flux &mdash; a labourer and sometimes butcher, and the first Haiste who really felt the pull of the city. In his father&rsquo;s day Leeds had been an unremarkable market town; by his middle age it was becoming a textile capital, and by the time his children were grown the family had spread into Leeds, into Hunslet, and into the woollen villages of the Aire valley &mdash; where one of them, a clothier named Joseph, would become the first Haiste whose existence rests not on a compilation but on the register of a parish church.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-joseph-haste-clothier-of-calverley-m-1802-the-fi"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>Joseph Haste, clothier of Calverley (m. 1802) &mdash; the first Haiste in the primary record</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">m. 1802 · Calverley → Idle, the Aire valley</p><p>The first man in this family whose existence is anchored by a primary register entry &mdash; not a compilation, but the ink-and-paper marriage book of a parish church &mdash; is a Joseph Haste who married Hannah Padget at the Church of St Wilfrid in Calverley on the fourth of January 1802. The register lists him as a <em>clothier</em> of Calverley, his bride as Hannah Padget of the same parish, the witnesses as Thomas Haste and Martha Padget. It has been preserved by the West Yorkshire Archive Service and transcribed on calverley.info. The entry reads, verbatim:</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch05-calverley-parish-village.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Yorkshire village gathered around its parish church at dusk" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024" /><figcaption>A Yorkshire parish church &mdash; where, in 1802, Joseph Haste&rsquo;s marriage first put the family into the written record. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The bedrock entry for this stretch of the family.</p></aside><aside class="heritage-line__quote"><p>1802-01-04 &mdash; HASTE, Joseph, Calverley, Clothier &mdash; PADGET, Hannah, Calverley &mdash; witnesses Thomas Haste, Martha Padget.</p></aside><p>This is the bedrock entry for this stretch of the family. Calverley is a village in the Aire valley northwest of Leeds, in the heart of Yorkshire&rsquo;s <em>woollen</em> district &mdash; distinct from the flax mills of Hunslet south of Leeds, where the inherited genealogy had wrongly placed a man of this generation (see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>). A clothier in 1802 produced woollen cloth &mdash; weaving on a cottage handloom, fulling, dyeing, finishing for market. Joseph and Hannah settled at Simpson Green, just outside Idle, in one of the last strongholds of the <em>domestic system</em>: families making cloth at home and selling finished pieces through the Piece Halls in Leeds and Halifax. The mills were coming for that trade, having already taken cotton, and by the time the couple&rsquo;s youngest children were born in the 1820s, the domestic clothier was a vanishing figure. But for the first two decades of the century, this was still the life that supported a family like theirs.</p><p>Their children, drawn from the Idle baptism registers, arrived at the steady cadence of a working household:</p><ul>
<li>Thomas &mdash; born 30 November 1802, almost certainly named for the witness at his parents&rsquo; marriage (likely Joseph&rsquo;s brother or father).</li>
<li>Nathan &mdash; born 24 June 1811.</li>
<li>Josiah &mdash; born 1 September 1813.</li>
<li><strong>Joseph</strong> &mdash; born 14 November 1815, baptised 29 March 1818. On the current evidence the most plausible bridge into the next generation.</li>
<li>William &mdash; born 12 February 1818.</li>
<li>Martha &mdash; born 3 February 1820.</li>
<li>Mary &mdash; 1822.</li>
</ul><p>Theirs was the world of Yorkshire textiles on the verge of full industrialization &mdash; the Napoleonic Wars keeping wool in high demand, and, in 1812, the Luddite uprisings, when West Riding wool workers smashed the mechanized cropping frames that were taking their livelihoods. Yorkshire was the heart of that movement, and Calverley sat a few villages over from the worst of the violence. The Haistes were part of the population whose hands made the cloth the British economy ran on, in the last years before the mills took the trade over.</p><p>Of the fourth-born Joseph &mdash; the 1815 child who carried his father&rsquo;s name &mdash; the verified record holds, for now, a single baptism entry; the censuses that should carry him toward John Haiste two generations later have yet to be searched (see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>). The story picks up again on solid ground with John.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-john-haiste-c-1840-after-1891-the-idea-of-leavin"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>John Haiste (c. 1840&ndash;after 1891) &mdash; the idea of leaving</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1840 · Leeds · Pontefract · Otley</p><p>John Haiste was born around 1840, into a Britain at the peak of its global power, the railway network already stitching Yorkshire together. He worked as a labourer, and, unlike his earliest ancestors, who had stayed in one or two parishes for generations, he moved, following work through Leeds, Pontefract (a mining and licorice town), and Otley (a mix of industry and farmland). He belonged to a generation that learned to be mobile: the railways made it possible to chase wages across a county, and the new culture of industrial labour made it normal. The old certainty that a man would live and die within sight of the fields he had played in as a child was already gone.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch07-victorian-working-family.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Victorian working-class family gathered in a humble cottage interior" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024" /><figcaption>A Victorian working household &mdash; John and Jessy Haiste raised eight children in one like it. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>What if we left?</p></aside><p>He married <strong>Jessy Hobson</strong> in late 1866, in the Wharfedale registration district near Otley &mdash; a Yorkshire woman of remarkable steadiness &mdash; and together they raised nine children, among them William, John Alfred, Aaron, Sarah Jane, Rose, Herbert, Phyllis, and <strong>Ernest</strong>. Victorian working mothers like Jessy ran households on razor-thin budgets and absorbed every shock the industrial economy threw at a family: illness, seasonal unemployment, accidents in the mills. The household ran on women like her.</p><p>John&rsquo;s own parentage is one of the open seams in this story. The most plausible candidate father is the Joseph born 1815 at Simpson Green, who would have been twenty-five when John was born, exactly the right age, but the link is not yet confirmed against a primary record (see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>).</p><p>What is not in doubt is what his lifetime contained: the shift that mattered most to the family&rsquo;s future. Emigration became normal. Steamships made the passage in days, the Canadian Pacific Railway (1885) opened the prairies, and posters appeared in every Yorkshire town advertising free homestead land. John never made the crossing, and most of his children stayed in Yorkshire. But the youngest son, Ernest, born in 1881, would. John&rsquo;s quiet contribution to the Haiste story is that he raised a son willing to ask the question that had begun to enter ordinary working-class English households for the first time in centuries &mdash; <em>what if we left?</em> &mdash; and to act on it.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-ernest-haiste-1881-1968-the-atlantic-crossing"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Ernest Haiste (1881&ndash;1968) &mdash; the Atlantic crossing</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1881&ndash;1968 · Yorkshire → Assiniboia, Saskatchewan</p><p>Ernest Haiste was born on 16 October 1881 in Pontefract, Yorkshire, into a world that was, for the first time, globally connected: the telegraph carrying news across oceans in minutes, steamships running on schedules, the Empire at its territorial peak. But the Empire was not for him. His choice &mdash; and it was a choice, made deliberately and not by economic accident &mdash; was to leave it.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch08-rms-baltic-steamship.jpg' ); ?>" alt="The RMS Baltic, a White Star Line ocean liner, at sea" loading="lazy" width="1597" height="1035" /><figcaption>The RMS Baltic &mdash; a White Star liner of the kind that carried Yorkshire emigrants from Liverpool to Halifax in the early 1900s.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>He did not just cross an ocean. He carried the entire future of the family with him.</p></aside><div class="heritage-longread__gallery"><p class="heritage-longread__gallery-title">The crossing west</p><div class="heritage-longread__gallery-grid"><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-homestead-sod-house.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Settlers in front of a prairie sod house" loading="lazy" width="400" height="211" /><figcaption>A prairie sod house &mdash; the kind of first home a homesteader like Ernest built by hand.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-homestead-barn-raising-1922.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Neighbours raising a barn frame, 1922" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="716" /><figcaption>A barn-raising, 1922 &mdash; neighbours travelled half a day by horse to get a barn up.</figcaption></figure><figure class="heritage-line__figure"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-cpr-rockies.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Engraving of a Canadian Pacific Railway train passing through the mountains" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1964" /><figcaption>The Canadian Pacific Railway through the mountains &mdash; the line that carried emigrants from Halifax to the prairie.</figcaption></figure></div></div><p>Around 1900 he boarded a steamship in Liverpool, crossed the Atlantic to Halifax, and passed through Canadian immigration into a country that was actively recruiting British and European homesteaders to populate the prairies west of Winnipeg. He boarded a Canadian Pacific train and rode it several days across nearly four thousand kilometres of forest, lake, and grassland to southern Saskatchewan: the Assiniboia district, where the Dominion government would grant a quarter-section (160 acres) to anyone who could break the soil, build a house, and stay three years. He stayed. He broke soil with his hands. He found a wife &mdash; whose name does not survive in the materials gathered here, though her presence anchors the next two generations &mdash; and together they raised <strong>Sydney</strong>, Herbert, Roy, Irene, and others: the first Haistes born on Canadian soil. The 1926 census still finds the family on the land in the Assiniboia district.</p><p>Life on a Saskatchewan homestead in those years was brutal and beautiful in roughly equal measure &mdash; endless sky and grass and wind, a small drafty house of sod or rough lumber, winters at forty below, water hauled by hand from a well that froze every November. But the soil was rich, the wheat grew in volumes Yorkshire farmers would not have believed, and the homestead came with something Yorkshire had never offered: the chance to own land. For a family whose recent ancestors had been tenant farmers, clothiers, and labourers &mdash; people who had spent generations selling their labour to someone else &mdash; a quarter-section of their own was a liberation no previous Haiste had known.</p><p>Ernest lived a long life, eighty-seven years, from 1881 to 1968 &mdash; long enough to watch horses give way to tractors and candles to lightbulbs, and to see his grandchildren grow up Canadian. He did not just cross an ocean. He carried the entire future of the family with him.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote"><p><strong>The convergence begins here.</strong> When Ernest broke his quarter-section of Assiniboia sod around 1907, he was not the first of this larger family to break Saskatchewan ground. A full generation earlier, in the 1880s, the cleared Hebrideans of Thomas Cheesman&rsquo;s line &mdash; the <strong>McIvers</strong> near Saltcoats, the <strong>Campbells</strong> near Wapella &mdash; had already turned the same prairie soil a few hundred kilometres to the east, and had already begun, as the Haistes soon would, to push on west to Alberta when the prairie would not hold them. Two crossings of the same dust, made decades apart by families who would never meet &mdash; until their descendants married a hundred and thirty years later, and the two prairie landfalls turned out to have been, all along, two tributaries of one river.</p></aside><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sydney-haiste-1906-1980s-the-dust-and-the-oilfie"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Five</span>Sydney Haiste (1906&ndash;1980s) &mdash; the dust and the oilfields</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1906&ndash;1980s · Saskatchewan → Alberta</p><p>Sydney Haiste was born on the Saskatchewan prairie in 1906 &mdash; the first generation of the family born outside England in three hundred years of recorded history. His was the working childhood of any prairie farm kid: chores started young, the land did not negotiate, and by his teens he was the kind of person who did not complain, did not quit, and did not wait for someone else to fix his problems.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/haiste/haiste-ch09-dust-bowl.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A dust storm rolling over a prairie settlement in the 1930s" loading="lazy" width="300" height="226" /><figcaption>A dust storm rolls over the prairie in the 1930s &mdash; the Dust Bowl that drove Sydney Haiste west to Alberta.</figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Don&rsquo;t look back, build here.</p></aside><p>Then the 1930s arrived, and the prairies fell apart. Wheat prices collapsed, crop failures stacked on each other, and the drought came, the worst in the prairies&rsquo; recorded history, followed by the Dust Bowl, the topsoil Ernest had spent thirty years working drying out and blowing away. Storms of fine black dust buried fences, killed livestock, and pushed thousands of homestead families off the land they had broken with their own hands. Sydney was in his late twenties through the worst of it, old enough to remember when the farm had been viable and young enough to watch it die.</p><p>So sometime in the late 1930s or 1940s he made the decision his father had made one generation earlier: he packed up and went looking for a better province. He went west, to Alberta &mdash; which, while Saskatchewan was buried in dust, was booming. Oil had been struck at Turner Valley before the First World War; the 1947 strike at Leduc, just south of Edmonton, would soon make the province the energy capital of the country; railways pushed north into Grande Prairie, and new refineries, pipelines, and oilfield towns appeared every year. He went where the work was, taking whatever trades came &mdash; farming, carpentry, the early oilfields, mill work &mdash; and built a life. He married, and raised a family that included his son <strong>Sidney James &ldquo;Jim&rdquo; Haiste</strong>, born in 1931 in the worst years of the Depression, and Jim&rsquo;s brothers Cliff, Bob, and Grant. The Haistes had now made two migrations in two generations (Yorkshire to Saskatchewan, Saskatchewan to Alberta), each driven by the same search for land or work that could feed the next generation, each asking the same thing: don&rsquo;t look back, build here.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-sidney-james-jim-haiste-1931-2018-the-alberta-pa"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Six</span>Sidney James &ldquo;Jim&rdquo; Haiste (1931&ndash;2018) &mdash; the Alberta patriarch</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1931&ndash;2018 · the Alberta patriarch</p><p>Jim Haiste was born on 23 April 1931 in Alberta, in the deepest pit of the Depression &mdash; a childhood of not enough money and a lot of teaching by example about how to make do with less. By the time he was a teenager the world had transformed: the Second World War had remade the Canadian economy, and Alberta, with the 1947 Leduc oil strike, was stepping into its modern identity at the exact moment Jim stepped into adulthood. His working life unfolded across the postwar boom.</p><p>He married twice. His first wife, Melanie&rsquo;s grandmother, was <strong>Margaret E. &ldquo;Betty&rdquo; Funnell</strong>, born about 1930 in Manitoba; together they raised their family across Dawson Creek, Edmonton, and Calgary through the postwar decades. After Betty, he married Catherine Caroline Barclay (1936&ndash;2016). Across the two marriages he raised six children: Patrick (m. Karen), Cynthia, Patricia, <strong>Daniel (&ldquo;Dan&rdquo;)</strong> &mdash; Melanie&rsquo;s father &mdash; Eric (m. Cheraty), and Chris. Many had children of their own, and by the 1990s the Haiste name was known from Edmonton to Grande Prairie; the roots Ernest had planted in Saskatchewan at the turn of the century had grown into branches that reached across a province.</p><p>Jim&rsquo;s life also held the kind of grief no parent expects to carry: he buried his son Chris, and he buried grandchildren. He carried those losses the way Alberta men of his generation tended to &mdash; quietly, without complaint, with the same posture handed down through generations of Haiste survival, from a Yorkshire clothier he had never heard of and a Yorkshire tanner who lived through plague and civil war four centuries before he was born. He died on 18 August 2018 in Edmonton, aged eighty-seven, having lived his last twenty-three years with Parkinson&rsquo;s disease; his funeral was held at St. Patrick&rsquo;s Anglican Church. He left children, grandchildren, and great-grandchildren.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-haiste-river-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Haiste river joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Grande Prairie · the confluence of five families</p><p>The Haiste line had no single word for what carried it through; it simply carried &mdash; four centuries deep, from a Yorkshire tannery to the Saskatchewan dust to the Peace Country &mdash; and it arrives, now, in the present.</p><div class="heritage-longread__filmreel" data-video-id="GVPemTqCjf8" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Haiste Family Line From Daniel on."><img class="heritage-longread__filmreel-thumb no-lightbox" src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/GVPemTqCjf8/maxresdefault.jpg" alt="Video: the Haiste family line, from Daniel on" loading="lazy" /><span class="heritage-longread__filmreel-play" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="heritage-longread__filmreel-cta">Watch: <strong>Haiste Family Line From Daniel on.</strong> · 1 h 11 m</span></div><p class="heritage-longread__filmreel-caption">The Haiste line on film &mdash; from Daniel onward, told by the family itself. From the family&rsquo;s channel, <em>Drifting Splash VIV</em>.</p><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"VideoObject","name":"Haiste Family Line From Daniel on.","description":"Haiste Family Line From Daniel on. — from the family's own channel, Drifting Splash VIV.","thumbnailUrl":"https://i.ytimg.com/vi/GVPemTqCjf8/maxresdefault.jpg","embedUrl":"https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/GVPemTqCjf8","contentUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GVPemTqCjf8","duration":"PT1H11M6S"}</script><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Five families, from four corners of the world, meeting at last in one Peace Country home.</p></aside><section class="heritage-tree" aria-label="The Haiste family descent, thirteen generations from a Yorkshire tannery to Grande Prairie"><h3 class="heritage-tree__title">The Haiste descent</h3><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Richard Haiste</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Elizabeth Haigh</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Barwick-in-Elmet</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>George</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>and almost certainly others</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>A yeoman and <strong>tanner</strong> &mdash; oak-bark pits, weeks of soaking and scraping, the indispensable filthy trade</li><li>Kept a household alive through the <strong>Civil War</strong>, the 1665 plague, and the Little Ice Age winters</li><li>His wife was <em>probably</em> Elizabeth Haigh &mdash; a family reconstruction, not yet a register entry; the generation is held <strong>inherited</strong></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">George Haiste Sr.</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Mary</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">bapt. 1641</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">William<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>George</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>John<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Elizabeth<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Richard<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Philip<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Mary<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Joseph<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Benjamin<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>and a George who died young</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Baptized in the spring of 1641 &mdash; the Civil War broke out the next year, and he grew up in the middle of it</li><li>A tenant farmer and <strong>butcher</strong> &mdash; two trades that fed each other and fed the village</li><li>Married a woman named Mary, her surname lost; parish reconstructions put their children at <strong>ten or more</strong></li><li>Died 1709, at sixty-eight &mdash; the name was never again at risk of dying out in Yorkshire</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">George Haiste</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">c. 1667&ndash;1743</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Georg</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Carried on the butchering and yeoman farming in the same <strong>Hunslet&ndash;Barwick</strong> parishes as his father and grandfather</li><li>The records of his wife and children run thinner here &mdash; the reconstructions place him solidly in that orbit</li><li>Died 1743, in the last quiet decades before the mills and railways</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Georg Haist</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">c. 1715</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>The spelling itself still in flux &mdash; <em>Georg Haist</em>, <em>George Haiste</em> &mdash; clerks entering the name by ear</li><li>A labourer and sometimes butcher; the first of the line who really felt the <strong>pull of the city</strong></li><li>By his children&rsquo;s day the family had spread into Leeds, Hunslet, and the <strong>Aire-valley woollen villages</strong> &mdash; where the primary record finally surfaces</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Joseph Haste</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Hannah Padget</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">m. Calverley 1802</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Thomas<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Nathan<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Josiah<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Joseph</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>William<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Martha<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Mary</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li><strong>The bedrock entry</strong>: married at St Wilfrid&rsquo;s, Calverley, 4 January 1802 &mdash; Joseph Haste, <em>clothier</em>; witnesses Thomas Haste and Martha Padget</li><li>The first Haiste anchored by a primary register rather than a compilation &mdash; West Yorkshire Archive Service originals</li><li>A domestic-system clothier at <strong>Simpson Green</strong>, outside Idle &mdash; cottage handloom wool sold through the Piece Halls, in the trade&rsquo;s last decades</li><li>The inherited &ldquo;Joseph born 1757, flax dresser of Hunslet&rdquo; was <strong>overturned</strong> when the registers were actually checked</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Joseph Haste Jr.</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">b. Simpson Green 1815</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born <strong>14 November 1815</strong>, baptised 29 March 1818 &mdash; the fourth-born of seven, carrying his father&rsquo;s name</li><li>What is directly verifiable is that baptism entry alone &mdash; tracking him into the 1841&ndash;1871 censuses is the biggest primary work left</li><li>On current evidence the most plausible bridge to the next generation &mdash; held as a <strong>hypothesis</strong>, and marked as such</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">John Haiste</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Jessy Hobson</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">m. Wharfedale 1866</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">William<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>John Alfred<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Aaron<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Sarah Jane<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Rose<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Herbert<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Phyllis<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Ernest</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>and one more</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>A labourer who <strong>moved</strong> &mdash; Leeds, Pontefract, Otley &mdash; in the first railway generation that could chase wages across a county</li><li>Married <strong>Jessy Hobson</strong> in late 1866 &mdash; Victorian households ran on women like her</li><li>His own parentage is an open seam: the 1815 Joseph is exactly the right age, but the link is <strong>not yet confirmed</strong></li><li>Never crossed himself &mdash; but raised the son willing to ask <em>what if we left?</em></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Ernest Haiste</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Pontefract → Assiniboia</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Sydney</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Herbert<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Roy<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Irene<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>and their siblings</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born Pontefract <strong>16 October 1881</strong>; around 1900 he boarded a steamship at Liverpool &mdash; and chose to leave the Empire</li><li>Broke a quarter-section of <strong>Assiniboia</strong> grass with his hands &mdash; the first Haiste land owned, not rented, in centuries</li><li>His wife&rsquo;s name does not survive in the materials gathered here &mdash; though her presence anchors the next two generations; the hunt is open</li><li>Lived to eighty-seven, 1881 to 1968 &mdash; long enough to watch horses give way to tractors and his grandchildren grow up Canadian</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Sydney Haiste</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Saskatchewan → Alberta</span><span class="heritage-tree__children"><strong>Jim</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Cliff<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Bob<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Grant</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born on the prairie in 1906 &mdash; the first generation born outside England in three hundred years of recorded history</li><li>Watched the <strong>Dust Bowl</strong> bury the farm his father had broken &mdash; and pulled the family west to booming Alberta</li><li>Took the work that came: farming, carpentry, the early oilfields, mill work</li><li>His own life is only lightly documented between the Saskatchewan farm and Alberta &mdash; a named chase in the Notes</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Sidney James &ldquo;Jim&rdquo; Haiste</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Margaret &ldquo;Betty&rdquo; Funnell</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Dawson Creek · Edmonton · Calgary</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Patrick<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Cynthia<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Patricia<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Dan</strong><span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Eric<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span>Chris</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born <strong>23 April 1931</strong>, in the deepest pit of the Depression; stepped into adulthood the year of the Leduc strike</li><li><strong>Betty Funnell</strong>, Melanie&rsquo;s grandmother, was Manitoba-born; after Betty he married Catherine Caroline Barclay</li><li>Six children across the two marriages; he buried his son Chris, and grandchildren &mdash; and carried it the way his generation did, quietly</li><li>Died in Edmonton, 18 August 2018, at eighty-seven, after twenty-three years with Parkinson&rsquo;s &mdash; St. Patrick&rsquo;s Anglican held the funeral</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Dan</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Lana Lyn Rycroft</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Grande Prairie</span><span class="heritage-tree__children">Misty<span class="heritage-tree__dot" aria-hidden="true"> &middot; </span><strong>Melanie</strong></span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born 1959 &mdash; the son through whom the Haiste name reaches the present</li><li>Married <strong>Lana Lyn Rycroft</strong> &mdash; the Rycroft, Steinke, and Thommessen currents join the Haiste one; her side is told in <a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/family/heritage/rycrofts/story' ) ); ?>">the Rycroft story</a></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Melanie</span><span class="heritage-tree__knot" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span><span class="heritage-tree__name">Thomas</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">m. 2016</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Married <strong>2 July 2016</strong> in Calgary</li><li>The parents of Patience, Daniel, and Faith</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="heritage-tree__gen"><div class="heritage-tree__couple heritage-tree__couple--facts" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="heritage-tree__names"><span class="heritage-tree__name heritage-tree__name--carrier">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span></span><span class="heritage-tree__note">Grande Prairie</span><span class="heritage-tree__more" aria-hidden="true">+</span><div class="heritage-tree__factswrap"><ul class="heritage-tree__facts"><li>Born 2013, 2015, and 2017, all in Grande Prairie</li><li>Heirs of all eight family lines &mdash; the confluence this whole site is built around</li></ul></div></div></div></section><p>Among Jim&rsquo;s descendants is his granddaughter <strong>Melanie Lyn Haiste</strong>, born in Grande Prairie in 1983 &mdash; the daughter of Jim&rsquo;s son <strong>Dan Steven Haiste</strong>, born in 1959, and the living point at which the whole Haiste line arrives in the present. Through Dan, and Jim, and Sydney, and Ernest, and the Calverley clothier, and the Yorkshire tanner before them all, she carries generations of Haiste survival: descendant of a tanner who lived through civil war and plague; of a clothier who married Hannah Padget two centuries before her and raised seven children at Simpson Green; of a man who crossed an ocean alone to break virgin Saskatchewan soil; of a man who pulled his family out of a dust storm and pointed them toward Alberta.</p><p>But Melanie does not carry the Haiste line alone, and this is where the shape of the whole family becomes clear. On her mother&rsquo;s side she carries the <strong>Rycrofts</strong> of Leeds and the <strong>Steinkes</strong>, through her mother Lana Lyn Rycroft. And in Calgary in 2016 (they married on the second of July) her life joined that of <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Calgary, who carries currents of his own: the <strong>Lakeman</strong> line out of a Dutch polder through his father, and the <strong>Docherty</strong> and Hebridean <strong>McIver</strong> lines out of Ireland and the Scottish islands through his mother. Thomas carries the surname Cheesman not by blood but by choice, from the step-father whose family gave it to him &mdash; the chosen channel into which all of these blood-lines pour. Melanie and Thomas are the parents of three children, all born in Grande Prairie: <strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong> (2013), <strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong> (2015), and <strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong> (2017). The Haiste line does not narrow to one of them. It passes to all three, together, braided with four other lines &mdash; the Lakeman, Docherty, and McIver through their father; the Haiste and Rycroft through their mother; and the Cheesman name that gathers them all. Five families, from four corners of the world, meeting at last in one Peace Country home.</p><p>None of those ancestors knew Patience, Daniel, or Faith. Richard Haiste could not have imagined an ocean; the Calverley clothier could not have imagined an aircraft; Ernest could not have imagined that his great-great-great-grandchildren would be born in a country whose grocery stores carried fruit from every continent, in towns connected to the whole earth by glass screens that fit in a hand. What every one of them did know &mdash; what every one of them practiced, generation by generation, without ever needing a single word for it &mdash; is the thing that brought their family through four centuries of plague, war, industry, migration, dust, and change.</p><p>They came through.</p><p>What Patience, Daniel, and Faith are handed is not the Haiste share alone but the confluence of all of it &mdash; proven where the records reach, and marked honestly where they don&rsquo;t. Four centuries of coming through, and the line begins again from here.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-lineage-at-a-glance">Lineage at a glance</h2><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
