</table></div><p><strong>Confidence tiers:</strong> <em>Verified</em> &mdash; anchored to at least one primary register or government record (parish register, civil registration, census, immigration or homestead file). <em>Inherited</em> &mdash; rests on the family genealogy without independent primary verification yet. <em>Living memory</em> &mdash; recent enough to be remembered directly by family.</p><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Richard</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1610&ndash;c. 1670</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">George Sr.</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1641&ndash;1709</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">George</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1667&ndash;1743</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Georg</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1715&ndash;?</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Joseph (clothier)</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">m. 1802</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Joseph</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1815</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">John</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1840</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Ernest</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1881&ndash;1968</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Sydney</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1906&ndash;1980s</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Jim</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1931&ndash;2018</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Dan</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1959</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Melanie</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1983</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">2013&ndash;2017</span></span></div><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>Side-branches and collateral kin named in the records but off the direct line: Joseph the clothier&rsquo;s other documented children at Idle &mdash; <strong>Thomas, Nathan, Josiah, William, Martha, and Mary</strong>; John Haiste&rsquo;s other children with Jessy Hobson (nine in all) &mdash; the seven recorded here being <strong>William, John Alfred, Aaron, Sarah Jane, Rose, Herbert, and Phyllis</strong>; Ernest&rsquo;s other Canadian-born children &mdash; <strong>Herbert, Roy, Irene</strong>, and their siblings; Sydney&rsquo;s other sons, Jim&rsquo;s brothers &mdash; <strong>Cliff</strong> (m. June), <strong>Bob</strong> (m. Audrey), and <strong>Grant</strong>; and Jim&rsquo;s wider family across two marriages &mdash; his first wife <strong>Margaret &ldquo;Betty&rdquo; Funnell</strong> (Melanie&rsquo;s grandmother; daughter of Thomas Ernest Funnell and Ethel Bastin, 1893&ndash;1975) and his second, <strong>Catherine Caroline Barclay</strong> (1936&ndash;2016), with children <strong>Patrick</strong> (m. Karen), <strong>Cynthia, Patricia, Eric</strong> (m. Cheraty), and <strong>Chris</strong> alongside Dan. Thirteen generations after a Yorkshire tanner kept his household alive through a civil war, the Haiste thread comes to rest at the foot of this table &mdash; three Alberta children in whom it joins the seven other lines, under the chosen Cheesman name.</em></p><details class="heritage-longread__notes" id="lr-notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>Like every family story that reaches back four centuries, the Haiste line is held together by records of vastly different quality. Some generations are documented to the day &mdash; Ernest&rsquo;s 1907 emigration paperwork, the modern Alberta years. Some rest on parish registers transcribed by volunteers. And the deepest seventeenth- and eighteenth-century reaches rest on family-tree compilations whose primary sources have not yet been independently checked. The story is solid at both ends; the middle is where the work remains.</p><h3>Key records still needed</h3><p>In rough order of value:</p><ol>
<li><strong>The link from Joseph (b. 1815) to John Haiste (c. 1840).</strong> The most consequential gap, because the entire Saskatchewan&ndash;Alberta line descends from John. Whether John was the son of the verified clothier&rsquo;s son, of some other Joseph, or of a man yet unidentified is the single most important piece of primary work left on the Yorkshire end.</li>
<li><strong>Joseph the clothier&rsquo;s own baptism</strong> &mdash; should appear in the Calverley registers around 1775&ndash;1782 if he was a native; it would name his parents and show whether his line connects to earlier Haistes in the area.</li>
<li><strong>The 1841 census for Calverley / Idle</strong> &mdash; would show whether the clothier survived past 1839 (the inherited death year for the supposed Hunslet flax dresser). If he is alive in Idle in 1841, the two men are definitively different.</li>
<li><strong>A burial record for Joseph the clothier</strong> &mdash; would settle his death year and place.</li>
<li><strong>The earlier Yorkshire generations.</strong> Richard, the early Georges, and Georg Haist all rest on the inherited genealogy; research in the Barwick-in-Elmet, Hunslet, and Leeds parish registers would put them on the same footing as the Calverley clothier.</li>
<li><strong>Sydney Haiste&rsquo;s life</strong> is only lightly documented between the Saskatchewan farm and Alberta &mdash; his birth, marriage, and movements are still to be filled in. And one date is genuinely unsettled: the family record places Ernest&rsquo;s emigration &ldquo;around 1900&rdquo; (he is farming in Assiniboia by the 1926 census), where an earlier account put the crossing nearer 1907.</li>
</ol><h3>Notes</h3><p><strong>The Chapter Two revision &mdash; the inherited &ldquo;Joseph born 1757,&rdquo; overturned.</strong> The biggest correction between drafts is here. The earlier genealogy described a Joseph Haiste born 1757, a flax dresser in Hunslet (south Leeds), died 1839, with a wife Hannah Padget and eight named children &mdash; a description drawn from the kind of online tree-compilations that propagate copy-and-paste errors across thousands of trees. When the primary Yorkshire parish records were actually checked (the Calverley/Idle transcriptions on calverley.info, from West Yorkshire Archive Service originals), a different man emerged: a <strong>clothier</strong>, not a flax dresser (woollen, not linen); of <strong>Calverley in the Aire valley</strong>, not Hunslet; <strong>married to Hannah Padget on 4 January 1802</strong>; with <strong>seven</strong> documented children at Idle/Simpson Green, 1802&ndash;1822. The dates make the inherited &ldquo;born 1757&rdquo; untenable &mdash; a man who married in 1802 and fathered children through 1822 was almost certainly born around 1775&ndash;1782. Two possibilities remain, and the evidence cannot yet decide between them: <strong>(a) conflation</strong> &mdash; the Calverley clothier and the inherited Hunslet flax dresser were two different men merged by a tree-builder, the clothier being the real ancestor; or <strong>(b) two unrelated families</strong> &mdash; the clothier is real but <em>not</em> ancestral to John Haiste, in which case the Hunslet flax dresser, if he existed at all, has yet to surface in any register. The verified Calverley family is the one this story follows; the inherited Hunslet cluster is set aside until it can be substantiated.</p><p><strong>Spelling.</strong> The family name was unstable for centuries &mdash; <em>Haist, Haiste, Haste,</em> and <em>Hayste</em> all appear for the same line, clerks entering it by ear. Normal for the period, and not evidence of separate families; but any future search must run all spellings.</p><p><strong>Where the line is solid.</strong> From John Haiste through Ernest, Sydney, Jim, and the modern Alberta family, the record rests on increasingly firm ground: late-Victorian English civil registration, Canadian immigration and homestead records, government records, and living memory.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>The story works at both ends. The middle is where the work remains.</em></p>
</div>
